  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜ぜ (masculine emphasis)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add ぜ (ze) at the end of a sentence for emphatic, masculine assertion. It expresses determination, excitement, or confidence. Think of it as a punchy 'I tell ya!' or 'let's do this!'\n\nExample from this song: The song uses ぜ throughout to convey high energy and determination — a very common particle in rock and punk songs.\n\nMore examples:\n• 行く (iku, 'to go') → 行くぜ (iku ze, 'I'm going!')\n• やる (yaru, 'to do') → やるぜ (yaru ze, 'I'll do it!')\n• いい (ii, 'good') → いいぜ (ii ze, 'It's good, man!')\n\nNote: ぜ is masculine casual speech. The feminine equivalents would be わ (wa) or の (no). In anime and songs, ぜ is extremely common for energetic male characters. It's stronger than よ (yo) but not as rough as ぞ (zo)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "行く (iku, 'to go') + ぜ (ze, emphasis) → 行くぜ (iku ze, 'I'm going!')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜たら (conditional)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add たら (tara) after the past tense form (た-form) of a verb to create a conditional — 'if/when X happens'.\n\nExample from this song: 引き寄せたら (hikiyosetara) = 'if you pull it close' — from 引き寄せる (hikiyoseru, 'to pull close') → 引き寄せた (hikiyoseta, past) + ら (ra) = 引き寄せたら (hikiyosetara)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べたら (tabetara, 'if you eat')\n• 会う (au, 'to meet') → 会ったら (attara, 'if you meet')\n• 暑い (atsui, 'hot') → 暑かったら (atsukattara, 'if it's hot')\n\nNote: たら is the most versatile conditional in Japanese. It works with verbs, adjectives, and nouns. For nouns/な-adjectives: だったら (dattara). It's especially natural for one-time or future events."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "引き寄せる (hikiyoseru, 'to pull close') → 引き寄せた (hikiyoseta, past) + ら (ra) → 引き寄せたら (hikiyosetara, 'if you pull it close')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜より (comparison)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place より (yori) after the thing being compared to express 'more than X' or 'rather than X'. The pattern is: A より B が [adjective] = 'B is more [adj] than A'.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses より to express preferences and comparisons — choosing one path over another.\n\nMore examples:\n• 昨日 (kinou, 'yesterday') → 昨日より元気 (kinou yori genki, 'healthier than yesterday')\n• 誰 (dare, 'anyone') → 誰よりも (dare yori mo, 'more than anyone')\n• 言葉 (kotoba, 'words') → 言葉より行動 (kotoba yori koudou, 'actions over words')\n\nNote: より can also mean 'from' in formal writing: 東京より (Toukyou yori, 'from Tokyo'). Adding も after より intensifies the comparison: 誰よりも強い (dare yori mo tsuyoi, 'stronger than anyone')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "昨日 (kinou, 'yesterday') + より (yori, 'than') → 昨日より (kinou yori, 'more than yesterday')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜だって (even if)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add だって (datte) after a noun, pronoun, or phrase to mean 'even' or 'even if'. It can also mean 'because' at the start of a sentence.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses だって to express 'even if' — showing that regardless of circumstances, the resolve remains unchanged.\n\nMore examples:\n• 僕 (boku, 'I') → 僕だって (boku datte, 'even I')\n• 何 (nani, 'what') → 何だって (nan datte, 'anything at all')\n• 子供 (kodomo, 'child') → 子供だって (kodomo datte, 'even a child')\n\nNote: だって has three uses: 1) 'even' after nouns (僕だって, 'even I'), 2) 'because' at the start (だって寒いもん, datte samui mon, 'because it's cold!'), 3) 'even if' after verbs in past tense (言ったって, ittatte, 'even if you say')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "僕 (boku, 'I') + だって (datte, 'even') → 僕だって (boku datte, 'even I')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜のさ (explanatory + masculine emphasis)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Add のさ (no sa) at the end of a sentence to explain something with a casual, confident tone. の (no) provides explanation, さ (sa) adds masculine assertion.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses のさ to assert its philosophy with rock-band confidence — 'that's just how it is, man'.\n\nMore examples:\n• そう (sou, 'so') → そうなのさ (sou na no sa, 'that's just the way it is')\n• 強い (tsuyoi, 'strong') → 強いのさ (tsuyoi no sa, 'I'm strong, you know')\n• 知ってる (shitteru, 'I know') → 知ってるのさ (shitteru no sa, 'I know, you see')\n\nNote: のさ is softer than んだぜ but still masculine and assertive. Common in song lyrics for declaring truths or personal philosophies. The feminine equivalent is のよ (no yo)."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "そう (sou, 'so') + なのさ (na no sa, 'it's that') → そうなのさ (sou na no sa, 'that's just how it is')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜っと (onomatopoeia emphasis)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: The っと (tto) ending is added to adverbs and mimetic words (onomatopoeia) to emphasize the manner or feeling of an action. The small っ adds a sharp, crisp quality.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses words like そっと (sotto, 'gently/quietly') and ギュッと (gyutto, 'tightly/squeezing') — mimetic adverbs with っと that vividly describe how actions are performed.\n\nMore examples:\n• じっと (jitto, 'still/staring intently')\n• ぐっと (gutto, 'with force / holding back emotions')\n• パッと (patto, 'suddenly/brightly')\n• ふわっと (fuwatto, 'softly/floatily')\n\nNote: Japanese has a rich system of mimetic words (擬態語, gitaigo). The っと pattern makes them feel more immediate and vivid. These words are essential for natural-sounding Japanese and are very common in everyday speech."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "そっと (sotto, 'gently') / ギュッと (gyutto, 'tightly') / じっと (jitto, 'intently')"
    }
  ]
